## Hybrid orchestration boundary

Cursor is the interactive implementation surface; GitHub is the repository
orchestration and governance surface.

For routine repository work, use this boundary:

1. Read and follow `AGENTS.md` and any applicable nested instructions.
2. Analyze, implement, refactor, debug, document, and run local deterministic
   validation in Cursor.
3. If the task produces repository changes, commit and push the
   implementation branch to GitHub and prepare the pull request.
4. Treat the pull request as the automated orchestration boundary. GitHub
   Actions runs deterministic CI.
5. Use GitHub check results and review findings as feedback. Fix confirmed
   defects in Cursor, push the update, and let GitHub rerun validation.
6. Leave merge eligibility and repository governance to GitHub rulesets,
   branch protection, required checks, and human decisions.

Invoke Antigravity, Grok Build, Copilot, or other specialists only when
`AGENTS.md` justifies escalation. Do not duplicate AI work Cursor has already
completed unless independent verification provides a concrete benefit.
Never commit secrets. Never put tokens in `mcp.json` or tracked docs.

Do not require direct Cursor credentials for local independent review.
Grok Build (`grok`) is the exclusive independent AI reviewer. Antigravity and
Antigravity CLI (`agy`) are secondary / specialist resources and are not an
independent-review fallback. Both roles are advisory. Assigned advice must be
recorded as a COMMENT-only pull-request comment or COMMENT review; Cursor may
post that attributed record when the reviewer cannot. Grok unavailability does
not transfer independent-review authority to Antigravity or another AI. Do not
copy vendor/model credentials or leftover GitHub Actions secrets into Cursor
configuration. This repository's GitHub Actions do not call xAI or Gemini.

Follow the shared escalation, cost, and default-implementation policy in
`AGENTS.md`. Do not restate those rules here. See the Agent hierarchy in
`AGENTS.md` rather than restating it here. Independent GitHub review findings
may be consumed as feedback in Cursor.

GitHub Actions and other deterministic checks remain authoritative for facts
they can verify. AI findings are advisory unless repository governance is
explicitly changed to make a corresponding check required.

## Cursor Cloud

These notes apply when the agent is running in Cursor Cloud. They do not
require other agents to use Cursor, and they do not replace GitHub as the
source of truth.

- Toolchain: `bash`, `python3`, `jq`, `node`/`npm`, and `shellcheck` are all
  provided by the base environment. `shellcheck` is a system dependency (apt),
  not a repo package, so it is baked into the environment rather than the
  startup update script. The update script only runs `npm ci` for the single
  `markdownlint-cli2` dev dependency.
- `markdownlint-cli2` is available via npm in this environment. It is not a
  CI gate; follow `AGENTS.md` and scope it to files you actually touch.
- Portable tests listed in `AGENTS.md` run here without AI370 hardware and
  without network. On generic cloud VMs, treat Stage 1/Stage 2 `WARN` results
  as recorded facts per `AGENTS.md`, not as a broken environment.

## GitHub MCP

Desktop Cursor loads [`.cursor/mcp.json`](../mcp.json). That file uses the
official hosted GitHub MCP endpoint `https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/`
with `X-MCP-Toolsets: default,projects`. Set `CURSOR_GH_PAT` in the
local environment to a token with repository access as needed, plus
`project` scope. Do not put tokens in `mcp.json` or `environment.json`.
Shared MCP architecture is
[`.github/github-mcp.md`](../../.github/github-mcp.md).

Cloud Agents do not take that PAT from `mcp.json`. Add the same HTTP server
in the Cloud Agent MCP dropdown or Dashboard → Integrations & MCP, then
enable it for this environment:

- URL: `https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/`
- Header: `Authorization: Bearer <CURSOR_GH_PAT>`
- Header: `X-MCP-Toolsets: default,projects`

`mcpServerAllowlist` only permits a server; it does not install headers or
authenticate it. This Cloud Agent environment is dashboard-managed
(personal). Do not commit `.cursor/environment.json` only to add an
allowlist: a committed file would override the dashboard environment.

If this environment later becomes repository-managed, allow the GitHub
server with:

```json
"mcpServerAllowlist": [
  {
    "name": "github",
    "serverUrl": "https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/"
  }
]
```
